# Shapes of Single Drops Rising Through ∗ Stagnant Liquids

Win MYINT<sup>∗∗</sup>, Shigeo HOSOKAWA<sup>∗∗</sup> and Akio TOMIYAMA<sup>∗∗</sup>
<sup>∗∗</sup>Kobe University, Graduate School of Science and Technology
1-1 Rokkodai, Nada, Kobe 657–8501, Japan
E-mail: tomiyama@mech.kobe-u.ac.jp

## Abstract

Shapes of single drops rising through infinite stagnant liquids under terminal conditions were measured using a high-speed camera for a wide range of fluid properties.
Aspect ratios *E* and distortion factors γ of drops were evaluated from drop images.
Effects of initial shape deformation, surfactants and viscosity ratio κ on *E* and γ were
investigated. As a result, the following conclusions were obtained. (1) Initial shape
deformation does not affect *E* and γ of single drops. (2) Addition of surfactants decreases *E* and breaks the fore-and-aft symmetry of a drop shape especially in systems
of low Morton numbers *M*. (3) The Tadaki number *Ta*is the best dimensionless number among the Eotv ¨ os number ¨ *Eo*, the Weber number *We* and *Ta* for correlating *E*
of drops, and an empirical correlation of *E* in terms of *Ta* was proposed for clean
drops under the conditions of −11.6 ≤ log *M* ≤−0.9, 1.5 × 10<sup>−2</sup> ≤ *Re* ≤ 8.5 × 10<sup>2</sup>,
1.7 × 10<sup>−2</sup> ≤ *Eo* ≤ 9.3, 7.4 × 10<sup>−3</sup> ≤ *Ta* ≤ 3.6and0.1 ≤ κ ≤ 100. (4) The viscosity
ratio κ does not affect *E* so much but influences γ.Forκ>1, γ decreases with increasing the drop diameter, that is, the front part of a drop is flatter than the rear part. On the
contrary, the rear part is flatter than the front part when κ<1. The fore-and-aft symmetry always holds for drops with κ ∼ 1.0. (5) Various drop shapes were presented as
a database utilized for verification of numerical methods such as an interface tracking
method and a boundary element method.

$$
M\leq-0.9,\;1.5\times10^{-2}\leq Re\leq8.5\times10^2
$$

$$
1.7\times10^{-2}\leq E o\leq9.3,7.4\times10^{-3}\leq T a\leq3.6
$$

$$
0.1\leq\kappa\leq100
$$

**_Key words_** : Drop, Shape, Aspect Ratio, Distortion Factor, Viscosity Ratio

## 1. Introduction

Shape of a fluid particle is of great importance in modeling particle motion and heat/mass
transfer through its interface. Grace et al.<sup>(1)</sup> proposed a shape regime map for fluid particles
rising or falling through stagnant liquids in terms of the Eotv ¨ os, Reynolds and Morton num-¨
bers, *Eo*, *Re* and *M*, which are defined by

$$
E o=\frac{F_{b}}{F_{\sigma}}=\frac{g d^{2}\left|\rho_{c}-\rho_{d}\right|}{\sigma}
$$

(1)

$$
Re=\frac{F_{i}}{F_{v}}=\frac{\rho_{c}V_{T}d}{\mu_{c}}
$$

(2)

$$
M=\frac{g\mu_c^4\left|\rho_c-\rho_d\right|}{\rho_c^2\sigma^3}
$$

(3)

where *F*<sub>b</sub>, *F*<sub>σ</sub>, *F*<sub>i</sub>, *F*<sub>v</sub>, g, *d*, ρ, µ, σ and *VT* are the buoyant force, the interfacial tension force,
the inertial force, the viscous force, the gravitational acceleration, the sphere-volume equivalent diameter of a fluid particle, the density, the viscosity, the interfacial tension and the terminal velocity, respectively. The subscripts *c* and *d* denote the continuous and dispersed phases,
respectively. Though their shape regime map provides qualitative information on shapes of
fluid particles, quantitative information on shapes such as surface area, projected area, and

$$
F_{b},F_{\sigma},F_{i},F_{v},g,d,\rho,\mu,\sigma
$$

$$
V_{T}
$$

