Several empirical correlations have been proposed for *E*. Wellek et al.<sup>(3)</sup> proposed the
following empirical correlation for non-oscillating drops in contaminated immiscible liquids:

$$
E=\frac{1}{1+0.091We^{0.95}}
$$

(6)

where *We* is the Weber number which is defined as the ratio of inertial force *F*<sub>i</sub>to interfacial
tension force *F*<sub>σ</sub>:

$$
We
$$

$$
\boldsymbol{F}_{i}
$$

$$
F_{\sigma}
$$

$$
We=\frac{F_{i}}{F_{\sigma}}=\frac{\rho_{c}V_{T}^{2}d}{\sigma}
$$

(7)

They also proposed the following correlation for low and moderate viscosity systems.

$$
E=\frac{1}{1+0.163E o^{0.757}}
$$

(8)

The following correlations of *E* for drops falling through air are given in the textbook written
by Clift et al.<sup>(2)</sup> :

$$
E=\frac{b_{1}+b_{2}}{2a}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}1.0&Eo\leq0.4\\\frac{1.0}{1.0+0.18\left(Eo-0.4\right)^{0.8}}&0.4<Eo<8\end{array}\right.
$$

(9)

Tadaki et al.<sup>(4)</sup> proposed the following correlations for shapes of single bubbles rising through
various stagnant liquids ( 3.94 × 10<sup>−11</sup> < *M* < 2.52 × 10<sup>−4</sup>).


$$
(3.94\times10^{-11}<M<2.52\times10^{-4})
$$

$$
\frac{d}{2a}=E^{1/3}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}0.62&16.5<Ta\\1.36Ta^{-0.28}&6<Ta<16.5\\1.14Ta^{-0.176}&2<Ta<6\\1&Ta<2\end{array}\right.
$$

(10)

where *Ta*is the Tadaki number defined by

$$
Ta=ReM^{0.23}
$$

(11)

---

Although these studies indicate that dimensionless numbers appropriate for correlating *E* may
be either *Eo*, *We* or *Ta*, the most appropriate dimensionless number for representing *E* of
drops in immiscible liquids has not been found out yet.

$E o,\:W e$

$$
Ta,
$$

(1)
Grace et al. pointed out that the viscosity ratio κ (=µ<sub>d</sub>/µ<sub>c</sub>)affects *E*, and proposed the
following empirical correlation for fluid particles in water:

$$
\kappa\left(=\mu_{d}/\mu_{c}\right)
$$

$$
E=\frac{1+0.15\kappa}{1+\kappa}E o
$$

(12)

Tomiyama et al.<sup>(5)</sup> pointed out that initial shape deformation (*I*.*S*.*D*.: shape deformation just
after the release of a fluid particle) affects *E* of single clean bubbles and a large *I*.*S*.*D*. results
in a small *E*. However, there is little information on the effects of κ and *I*.*S*.*D*. on *E* of single
drops.

(2), (7) – (12)
Experimental and numerical studies on the distortion factor γ or the fore-andaft symmetry of a drop shape have been also conducted. These researches have indicated the
importance of not only external flow field but also internal circulation in a drop. This, in turn,
implies that the viscosity ratio κ would affect γ. There is, however, few experimental data on
γ, and therefore, the effects of κ, *I*.*S*.*D*. and surfactants on γ have not been clarified yet.

In this study, shapes of single drops rising through infinite stagnant liquids under terminal
conditions were measured by using a high-speed camera for a wide range of fluid properties.
The aspect ratio *E* and distortion factor γ of a drop were evaluated from drop images. Effects
of initial shape deformation, surfactants and viscosity ratio κ on *E* and γ were discussed.

## 2. Experiment

Experimental apparatus and instrumentations as shown in Fig. 2 were the same as those
(13), (14)
used in our previous experiments. Shapes of drops rising through stagnant liquids in
a tank were recorded by using a high-speed video camera (Kodak Motion Corder Analyzer
SR-1000, shutter speed = 1/5000 s, frame rate = 250 frame/s, 512×480 pixels). The size
of the tank was 0.20, 0.20 and 0.69 m in width, depth and height, respectively. Silicon oil
(Sinetsu Silicone, KF96-30 or KF96-100) was used for drops. Purified water or glycerolwater solutions were used for the continuous phase. Experiments were conducted at room
temperature and atmospheric pressure. Experimental conditions are summarized in Table 1.
A drop was released from a nozzle by pushing a cylinder of a syringe, which was connected
to the nozzle as shown in Fig. 2. Various nozzles with different inner diameters (*DN* =
1.2 − 8.0 mm) were used to release single drops of various sizes and to alter the degree of
initial shape deformation (large initial shape deformation occurs when *d* is larger than *DN*).
For experiments in contaminated systems, Triton X-100 (C₈H₁₇C₆H₄(OCH₂ CH₂)<sub>10</sub>OH) was
used for surfactants, the concentration of which was 2.68×10<sup>−1</sup> mol/m³. Note that the addition
of Triton X-100 decreases σ and increases *M*, e.g., the fluid system of σ = 49.6 × 10<sup>−3</sup> N/m
and log *M* = −4.8 changed to that of σ = 28.8 × 10<sup>−3</sup> N/mandlog*M* = −4.1. Changes in σ
and *M* were small in the case of a high *M* system (e.g., log *M* = −3.0).

