## 3. Results and discussions

## 3.1. Effects of I.S.D., surfactants and viscosity ratio on drop shape

The influence of initial shape deformation (*I*.*S*.*D*.) on drop shapes was examined using
(13), (14)
various nozzles with different inner diameters. In Fig. 3(a), measured aspect ratios *E*
in clean and contaminated systems are plotted against *Eo*. For comparison, measured *E<sup>(14)</sup>*
of single bubbles in the same Morton number system is shown in Fig. 3(b). There is a large
scatter in *E* of bubbles in the clean system due to the difference in *I*.*S*.*D*., and the scatter
is small in the contaminated system because of a large decay of shape oscillation caused by
surfactants<sup>(5)</sup>. On the other hand, the scatter in *E* of drops is small not only in the contaminated
system but also in the clean system. This might be because the viscous damping of shape
oscillation for single drops is higher than that for single bubbles in clean systems. The effect
of *I*.*S*.*D*. on γ is similar to that on *E* as shown in Fig. 4. That is, the scatter in γ is small
for both clean and contaminated drops (Fig. 4(a)), whereas the scatter in γ of clean bubbles
is large (Fig. 4(b)). These experimental data indicate that *I*.*S*.*D*. does not affect *E* and γ of
single drops.

