Figure 5 shows measured *E* of single drops in clean and contaminated systems under
three different Morton number conditions. As shown in Fig. 5(a), surfactants do not affect
*E* in the large *M* and small κ system. This is because the effect of surfactants on interfacial
tension σ is negligible in the log *M* = −3.0 system. On the contrary, *E* of a contaminated drop
in small *M* and large κ systems is smaller than that of a clean drop as shown in Fig. 5(b) and
(c). Stone and Leal<sup>(6)</sup> also reported that the adsorption of surfactants on interface causes larger
deformation. Figure 6 shows measured γ in clean and contaminated systems under the three
conditions. Though the difference in γ between clean and contaminated systems is small in
log *M* = −3.0 (Fig. 6(a)), the results in the lower *M* systems (Fig. 6(b) and (c)) indicate that
γ of a clean drop takes a value closer to unity than that of a contaminated drop with the same
diameter, that is, clean drops have better fore-and-aft symmetry than contaminated drops.
This is due to the larger deformation (small *E*) caused by the surfactant-induced decrease in
σ. Note that it depends on the fluid properties whether γ is larger than unity or not. These
results indicate that the influences of surfactants on *E* and γ are mainly caused by the decrease
in σ due to the adsorption of surfactants.

