Then the data are plotted against *We*in Fig. 10(a) and (b) together with Eq. (6) proposed
by Wellek et al.<sup>(3)</sup>. The measured *E* monotonously decreases with increasing *We*,andthe
scatter of data in Fig. 10 is small compared with that in Fig. 9. Therefore, *We* is one of
appropriate dimensionless numbers for correlating *E*. Equation (6) overestimates *E* except
for drops in the case of (log *M*,κ) = (−11.6, 100).

$$
M,\kappa)=(-11.6,100)
$$

Relations between *E* and *Ta*in clean and contaminated systems are shown in Fig. 11(a)
and (b), respectively. Since the scatter of data in Fig. 11(a) is slightly smaller than that in
Fig. 10(a), we can confirm that *Ta* is the more appropriate dimensionless number than *We*
for correlating *E*. The broken curves in Fig. 11 correspond to Eq. (10) proposed by Tadaki et
al. and the alternate long and short dash curves are drawn by using the following correlation
proposed by Vakrushev & Efremov<sup>(15)</sup>.


$$
E=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}1&(Ta\leq1)\\\left[0.81+0.206\tanh\left\{2\left(0.8-\log_{10}Ta\right)\right\}\right]^3&(1\leq Ta\leq39.8)\\0.24&(Ta\geq39.8)\end{array}\right.
$$

 1(
(13)

Since these correlations have been developed for bubbles, they cannot accurately evaluate *E*
of drops. We therefore developed the following empirical correlation for single drops in clean
systems:

$$
E=1-0.0487Ta-0.0289Ta^{2}
$$

(14)

