## 3.3. Distortion factors in various systems

Distortion factors γ of single drops in clean and contaminated systems are plotted against
*Eo* in Fig. 12 together with Clift’s correlation of γ for drops in air<sup>(2)</sup>.


$$
\frac{b_{2}}{b_{1}+b_{2}}=\frac{\gamma}{2}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}0.5&E o\leq0.5\\ \frac{0.5}{1.0+0.12\left(E o-0.5\right)^{0.8}}&0.5<E o<8\end{array}\right.
$$

(15)

The measured γ ranges from 0.85 to 1.2 whereas Eq. (15) gives a value lower than unity. The
γ takes a larger value than unity for κ<1, whereas it tends to be less than unity for κ>1.
When κ is close to unity, γ also takes a value close to unity, that is, when κ ∼ 1, a drop keeps
the fore-and-aft symmetry irrespective of *Eo*. As shown in Fig. 12(a), under clean conditions
with κ<1, γ can be approximately evaluated by

$$
\gamma=0.957+0.008Eo+0.002Eo^{2}
$$

