Figure 13(a) shows the relation between γ and *We* in clean systems. The measured
γ in systems of large *M* and small κ [(log *M*,κ)=(−3.0, 0.4), (−0.9, 0.1)] increases with *We*
(γ>1). However, γ in systems of moderate *M* and κ [(log *M*,κ) = (−4.8, 0.9), (−3.0, 1.1)]
keeps a value close to unity. That is, though drop shape changes from spherical to ellipsoidal,
it keeps the fore-and-aft symmetry. On the contrary, γ in systems of small *M* and high κ
[(log *M*,κ)=(−6.0, 1.9), (−11.6, 31.5), (−11.6, 100)] decreases as *We* increases. Namely, a
spherical drop deforms and its front part becomes flat. This difference in dependence of γ
on *We* between fluid systems would be caused by the difference in internal circulation in a
(2), (7) – (12)
drop. Since internal circulation is induced by viscous stress acting on interface, κ
may play a more important role in the dependence of γ on *We* than *M*. As shown in Fig.
13(b), γ of contaminated drops shows the same trend as that of clean drops. These results
indicate that γ does not depend on *M* but on κ, and that it is smaller than unity for κ>1and
larger than unity for κ<1.

## 3.4. Summary of shapes on Grace diagram

Figure 14 shows the relation between *Re* and *Eo* of clean drops together with representative drop shapes. Experimental data for drops with fluctuating motion are also included for
reference. The solid and broken curves are drawn by using drag correlations for clean and
contaminated bubbles proposed by Tomiyama et al.<sup>(16)</sup>. The alternate long and short dash
curves correspond to an empirical correlation proposed by Win et al.<sup>(13)</sup>.The*Re* increases
and drop shape deforms from spherical to ellipsoidal as *Eo* increases. Drop shapes obtained
in the present study are also summarized in Appendix. These shape data can be utilized for
verification of numerical methods such as interface tracking and boundary element methods.

